# Covers: Lobby.wall is a BROADCAST.  Session 1's wall reaches session 2's
#         output and does NOT reach session 1's own, which is the whole
#         difference between wall and echo.  The overlay in stdlib.u
#         delivers each copy through __builtin_lobby_send_to, so the frame
#         is written on the TARGET realm's writer.
#
#         Two sessions, so `Lobby.lobbies` has two entries and the skip of
#         the caller's own lobby is observable.  wall_broadcasts.chk with
#         its single session can only show that the registry exists.
# matrix-row: lobby-wall

## mode: repl

@sessions=2

> {"id":1,"op":"eval","code":"Lobby.lobbies.length()"}
< "id":1 | "kind":"result" | "value":"2"
< "id":1 | "kind":"done"

# Session 1 broadcasts.  Session 2 sees an output envelope carrying no id,
# because the write did not happen inside ITS eval frame.
> {"id":2,"op":"eval","code":"Lobby.wall(\"hello-all\", \"\")"}
< "id":2 | "kind":"result"
< "id":2 | "kind":"done"
<2 "kind":"output" | *** hello-all

# And session 1 got nothing but its own two envelopes: an unclaimed
# response at end of fixture is a failure, so a copy delivered to the
# sender would fail this fixture rather than pass unnoticed.
